WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:11.530
Our panel today looks at the chill effect.

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:15.940
We've all had it, that moment when you hear
something in a piece of music, and shivers

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:20.150
run down your spine or the little hairs on
the back of your neck stand up.

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:46.240
Mari's piece, Alt has a kind of ghostly quality,
which is why she's chosen it for us today.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:01:11.450
Mari is obviously not the only person that
will be on our little panel, our discussion

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:12.450
today.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:19.010
Let me introduce you to the rest of the gang.Beginning
with Psyche Loui, she is Assistant Professor

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:24.979
currently at Wesleyan University, but about
to start taking over in the Music and Creativity

00:01:24.979 --> 00:01:33.229
department at Northeastern University, please
welcome Psyche Loui.

00:01:33.229 --> 00:01:38.719
And Professor of Psychology and Neural Science
at NYU, David Poeppel.

00:01:38.719 --> 00:01:46.630
Assistant Professor of Psychology at SUNY,
Purchase, Meagan Curtis.

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:56.850
From the University of Connecticut, Professor
of Physics and Psychology, Ed Large.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:02.530
Psyche you are also a violinist, so you would've
known all the technical stuff that was going

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:05.539
into Mari's piece.

00:02:05.539 --> 00:02:11.450
But, was it ghostly sounding, and if so, why?

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:14.569
What would make a piece have that kind of
character to it?

00:02:14.569 --> 00:02:20.599
I thought it was ghostly sounding, and I think
to me it comes down to the fulfillment and

00:02:20.599 --> 00:02:22.120
violation of expectations.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:27.950
So, I think the piece had a lot of dissonance,
had a lot of bow noise, and I think our brains

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:34.790
by this age, we're primed to think about sounds
as being consonant, especially in music.

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:43.220
And so, when you add a little bit more noise
to the bow, or you play closer to the bridge,

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:50.400
that gives you that shivery feeling that is
dissonance, and I think that that's what makes

00:02:50.400 --> 00:03:13.610
a piece feel kind of unexpected and eerie.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:18.849
And I think it's that interplay between the
unexpected and then kina more consonant resolutions

00:03:18.849 --> 00:03:22.250
of sounds that makes us get the chills.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:28.800
Ed, you do a lot of studying of this vibration
oscillation waveform, that sort of thing.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:29.800
Right.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:34.329
It can actually have the opposite effect of
putting people off of kind of distancing them

00:03:34.329 --> 00:03:36.530
from what they're hearing, right?

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:37.790
Sure, sure.

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:42.750
One of things, obviously expectation is very
important, but one of the things that happens

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:48.849
when you listen to those kind of sounds is,
or any sound, is that the brain stem, the

00:03:48.849 --> 00:03:56.439
early parts of the auditory system, literally
are synchronizing with the sound on a cycle-by-cycle

00:03:56.439 --> 00:04:03.310
basis, and that has really ... It can make
even a single sound, not even two sounds in

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:09.360
a row, but those kind of harmonics that Mari
was doing, very emotional, and really give

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:13.340
you the chills just that one note even.

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:19.550
But it will be a very individual response,
what works for one person, will not work for

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:20.550
another.

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:27.449
When Meagan I know you really kind of have
worked a lot with targeting how individuals

00:04:27.449 --> 00:04:31.560
respond to things like music, right?

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.449
To a certain extent.

00:04:33.449 --> 00:04:38.510
There is a lot of individual variability with
how people respond to certain songs.

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:43.740
So a song that might create an intensely emotional
experience for me, may not create any sort

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:46.360
of experience for you.

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:52.650
And is there a way of ... Has neuroscience
gotten to the point where it can kind of co-defy

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:59.639
what the parameters are that make it likely
for one kind of music to affect a person that

00:04:59.639 --> 00:05:03.530
way, as opposed to another?

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:11.860
Well, I think that there are some predictors
that can tell you who is most likely to respond

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:13.550
emotionally to a certain type of piece.

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:18.470
So for instance, if you have a personal history
with the piece, if it's well-known to you,

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:22.110
if you're a musician, if you've played the
piece before, that's going to create a deeper

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:28.569
emotional response, and the context in which
it occurs is going to influence how you respond

00:05:28.569 --> 00:05:30.300
to the music.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:38.039
So there are some things that do seem to have
predictive value, however there's still tremendous

00:05:38.039 --> 00:05:40.350
variability across different individuals.

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:41.350
Right.

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:44.910
There's a lot there that we're gonna unpack
during the course of this conversation.

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:48.150
Things like context, expectation.

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:57.150
David, it seems like maybe 25/30 years ago,
music and neuroscience seemed like distinct

00:05:57.150 --> 00:06:04.140
fields, and then gradually in the wake of
Oliver Sacks' work, neuroscience began to

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:09.110
kinda look at music, and then really focus
on music, and now it's like, obsessed with

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:10.669
music.

00:06:10.669 --> 00:06:18.879
What has your experience been of this kind
of progression of interest on the part of

00:06:18.879 --> 00:06:20.090
neuroscientists in this art.

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:25.330
And first, we have to of course say it, neuroscience
is obsessed with everything, 'cause neuroscience

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:27.220
can explain everything.

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:31.090
I have to say that as Professor of Neuroscience.

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:38.970
But it's true that the one big change has
been the availability of techniques to measure

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:41.800
the human brain, that weren't available 25
years ago.

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:48.560
In the last 25 years, we've kind of optimized
and developed brain recording techniques from

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:53.140
awake behaving humans, listening to music,
listening to speech, that allow us to monitor

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:58.460
what's going on and begin to come up with
kind of more satisfying mechanistic answers.

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:04.789
Some of us work on this, and of course it's
often much more compelling and fun to study

00:07:04.789 --> 00:07:08.830
something like music than the usual stuff
we do in the lab, which is you come to my

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:14.460
lab, you sit in a machine and you hear beep,
beep, beep, and then 90-minutes later you're

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:17.150
still hearing beep.

00:07:17.150 --> 00:07:21.139
That is necessary, that's how we sort of build
up the parts list of how a mind and brain

00:07:21.139 --> 00:07:25.289
are organized, but in the end, we're interested
in compelling experiences, like music.

00:07:25.289 --> 00:07:27.440
Why do we find Mari's piece mesmerizing?

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:31.240
Not because it's beep, beep, beep, because
it's something other in our head that's going

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:32.240
on.

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:33.240
And that's what we're trying to figure out.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:42.250
So if you're able to take an FMRI for example,
a functional MRI of a brain as it's in the

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:48.940
act of listening to music, one the interesting
things is music seems to affect the brain

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:54.699
globally, there is no like one part of the
brain as there is for speech.

00:07:54.699 --> 00:07:57.030
Music seems to be all over the place.

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:59.970
Yeah, so let me disagree with you already.

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:00.970
Okay.

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:04.629
So the same is true for speech.

00:08:04.629 --> 00:08:07.050
Speech is just as complicated as music.

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:10.159
Let's go right into the meat of this, right?

00:08:10.159 --> 00:08:13.409
You asked Psyche, why is it ghostly?

00:08:13.409 --> 00:08:19.449
Well, there is a huge individual differences
which we're gonna talk about and Psyche has

00:08:19.449 --> 00:08:24.030
actually a very influential recent paper about
the wiring diagram that is.

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:30.250
When I listen to it, my individual, my response
it was, it reminded me of animals.

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:34.350
You probably had nothing like that in mind,
your dog or some bird, but I thought this

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:38.680
was a scene of many different animal sounds
coming together in weird ways, and that was

00:08:38.680 --> 00:08:40.310
my interpretation of it.

00:08:40.310 --> 00:08:45.470
So in the privacy of my own mind, I'm entitled
to interpret music any way I like.

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:46.790
This is not the case for speech.

00:08:46.790 --> 00:08:54.010
In the case of speech, meaning is conveyed
by putting things together, you pull out particular

00:08:54.010 --> 00:09:00.310
words, and then you put the words together
to construct meaning, and that is much more

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:02.510
directed in some sense, than it is in the
musical case.

00:09:02.510 --> 00:09:08.630
So we have our own private interpretation
much more so, and that's partly tremendous

00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:09.649
individual variability.

00:09:09.649 --> 00:09:14.940
So, if I say to you, man bites dog.

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:19.750
All six of us will have the same interpretation
of what that means, as will the rest of you,

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:22.290
and if you don't come see me later.

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:26.790
But if you hear, even a few bars of music,
you will not.

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:32.620
And therein lies a really big difference,
and that big difference is carried by pretty

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:35.960
complicated networks that are true for the
speech case, and the music case.

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:37.899
So it's a story.

00:09:37.899 --> 00:09:40.410
Well, I think yes and no, right?

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:45.630
I think that it's a bit of a strong statement
to think that if you have one piece of music

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:47.760
that we don't all hear it the same way.

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:52.360
I think we can all take different emotional
interpretations from it, but I think part

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:58.280
of the goal of composition in a way, is to
align a composer's intention with the audiences

00:09:58.280 --> 00:09:59.280
attentions.

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:03.620
Otherwise, if there's no alignment, and this
is from Fred just down the street, if there's

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:07.050
no alignment, you could say that a piece is
kind of failing.

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:09.029
And so, we have to find the common ground.

00:10:09.029 --> 00:10:14.149
Musicians have to find the common ground between
what we're making and what the audience is

00:10:14.149 --> 00:10:19.100
hearing, and I think that comes down to like
us having similarly wired brains to a certain

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:22.110
extent, but of course there's lots of differences.

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:26.810
I think that it's not so simple that you just
can't take whatever you want out of music,

00:10:26.810 --> 00:10:29.160
I think that there are some commonalities.

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:34.920
And coming from the other side, 'cause I'm
on your other side.

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:37.670
It's not as though language is that simple
either.

00:10:37.670 --> 00:10:44.280
I mean, man bites dog is one thing, but a
more complicated sentence, a more complicated

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:48.380
discourse, people interpret in many different
ways also.

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:53.779
And that's one of the interesting things about
language and music, is it's got this multifaceted

00:10:53.779 --> 00:10:56.410
kind of meaning that we can see.

00:10:56.410 --> 00:11:01.279
Well coming from the other side, the other-other
side, as an interpreter, I do classical whatever,

00:11:01.279 --> 00:11:09.050
and if you're trained in the violin, you know
that you have to practice the same thing over

00:11:09.050 --> 00:11:15.060
and over and over and over and over again,
until you're technically, right?

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:21.800
So how do you keep yourself fresh, and how
do you present yourself emotionally coherent

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:25.850
to the audience so that you're not just tracing
your notes?

00:11:25.850 --> 00:11:28.269
What I do, personally this is like behind
the-

00:11:28.269 --> 00:11:32.730
Tell us how the sausage is made, go ahead.

00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:36.640
Industrial secret that you're not supposed
to know.

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:38.870
No, just my own process.

00:11:38.870 --> 00:11:43.589
I use the Stanislavski, like a, actor's method
act-

00:11:43.589 --> 00:11:44.589
Method acting.

00:11:44.589 --> 00:11:45.589
Yeah, method acting.

00:11:45.589 --> 00:11:52.290
I read a lot of Stanislavski when I was young,
and I built my own emotional sub context,

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:53.290
right?

00:11:53.290 --> 00:12:00.519
So I learned early on that if I practice and
practice, I am just bored on stage, because

00:12:00.519 --> 00:12:01.779
I've done this before.

00:12:01.779 --> 00:12:04.780
And if I'm bored, you are bored, right?

00:12:04.780 --> 00:12:11.720
So for me not too bored, I have to build this
sub context of emotions so that I can follow

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:12.720
that thing.

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:19.430
But then I have to choose a subtext maybe
a day or two before, maybe a conversation

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:28.149
I had, a book I read, a news I saw, some story
that I kind of lay out emotionally, and then

00:12:28.149 --> 00:12:30.290
that way I can present that.

00:12:30.290 --> 00:12:36.500
But then you are not seeing that, you don't
know the book I read, but emotional logic

00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:39.000
is there, so you can follow the logic.

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:40.262
That's how I do it.

00:12:40.262 --> 00:12:46.650
I wanna come back to this idea that if you
as a performer or as a composer, aren't feeling

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:48.980
it, that the audience won't feel it.

00:12:48.980 --> 00:12:55.160
Because there was a study done some years
ago, about job satisfaction, and orchestral

00:12:55.160 --> 00:13:02.610
musicians ranked slightly below sanitation
workers.

00:13:02.610 --> 00:13:11.350
I'm sorry if this has come as a shock to you.

00:13:11.350 --> 00:13:16.889
I'm thinking about ... Oh my God.

00:13:16.889 --> 00:13:24.910
The fact that we can go to an orchestral concert,
and have a really amazing experience, which

00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:35.050
is being played to us, apparently by 104 people
who are bored out of their tiny minds, is

00:13:35.050 --> 00:13:36.860
a little weird.

00:13:36.860 --> 00:13:38.079
Yeah.

00:13:38.079 --> 00:13:47.110
And we've each picked a piece of music to
share with you today that for each of us,

00:13:47.110 --> 00:13:53.360
gives us that shiver, that chill effect, and
Psyche you picked an orchestral piece.

00:13:53.360 --> 00:14:01.490
Does it matter to you if you know that that
orchestra has been sawing through this piece

00:14:01.490 --> 00:14:08.830
all week long, and is sick of the sound of
it, and they're just doing what ... It's a

00:14:08.830 --> 00:14:09.830
job.

00:14:09.830 --> 00:14:10.830
Does that-

00:14:10.830 --> 00:14:11.830
Right.

00:14:11.830 --> 00:14:15.440
I actually almost chose to become an orchestral
musician, and then didn't.

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:19.529
And I think it is because of this feeling
that if you're doing the same thing over and

00:14:19.529 --> 00:14:22.850
over again each day, it might get boring instead
of being the thing that you love.

00:14:22.850 --> 00:14:27.660
But I don't think that's completely true,
I think that my friends who choose to go into

00:14:27.660 --> 00:14:31.680
music professionally, they choose it not because
they're not good at anything else it's just

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:35.380
that they can't imagine dedicating their life
to something else.

00:14:35.380 --> 00:14:40.980
Which I think, says something about why music
is so important, because it's doing something

00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:46.110
to you emotionally, and it's triggering your
reward system, it's hopefully good for your

00:14:46.110 --> 00:14:49.720
brain, there's lots of work on that as well.

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:56.130
But I think at the end of the day, even when
a bored orchestral musician is performing

00:14:56.130 --> 00:15:02.470
onstage in a concert, I think they do enter
a different world that is professional and

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:06.449
is together and is kind of in it for the greater
good.

00:15:06.449 --> 00:15:13.839
I think that the other stuff is maybe like
union, administration, all these other things

00:15:13.839 --> 00:15:17.510
that
Well I think there's also a question of agency.

00:15:17.510 --> 00:15:21.620
Musicians like to make the music that they
want to play, and when you're an orchestral

00:15:21.620 --> 00:15:26.040
musician, you play the music you're told to
play, in the way that the conductor at his

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:30.699
or her tempo, and you're waiting for your
cue, et cetera.

00:15:30.699 --> 00:15:32.230
So there is a question of agency.

00:15:32.230 --> 00:15:37.279
I mean, with all due respect to orchestral,
I know many of them, and they're deeply committed,

00:15:37.279 --> 00:15:43.459
but there is that, they're not getting that
little pop that you're getting when you hear

00:15:43.459 --> 00:15:44.459
this piece.

00:15:44.459 --> 00:15:47.079
Explain what this piece does for you.

00:15:47.079 --> 00:15:54.029
So Rachmaninoff Second Piano Concerto, Second
Movement, and I chose the beginning of it.

00:15:54.029 --> 00:16:13.990
It's really, let's just listen to that.

00:16:13.990 --> 00:16:19.900
So it's kind of a hushed tremulous opening
there.

00:16:19.900 --> 00:16:25.410
Yeah, I think it's setting up a lot of action
that's about to happen.

00:16:25.410 --> 00:16:29.721
And I think that feeling of anticipation,
there's also a lot of chord changes, and we

00:16:29.721 --> 00:16:37.009
know from Joss Lebotta's work in '89/'91 that
chord changes are more likely to elicit that

00:16:37.009 --> 00:16:41.880
feeling of like as if your heart's skipping
a beat.

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:47.350
Whereas melodic changes in certain ... There's
music theoretical predictors for how you make

00:16:47.350 --> 00:16:51.449
these melodic changes, but those are more
likely to make people cry.

00:16:51.449 --> 00:16:57.699
So you have different kinds of strong emotional
responses that are triggered by different

00:16:57.699 --> 00:16:59.690
kind of compositional devices.

00:16:59.690 --> 00:17:05.620
Which is why I think that ... and I think
that composers' kind of implicitly know about

00:17:05.620 --> 00:17:11.530
these types of devices, and that's why they're
able to deploy these tools of music theory

00:17:11.530 --> 00:17:15.080
to make a rewarding experience for audience.

00:17:15.080 --> 00:17:22.820
When you get to things like rhythm, you're
getting back, aren't you to the lizard brain,

00:17:22.820 --> 00:17:34.400
the brainstem and the fact that we learn early
on to entrain to a beat, so it sort of comes

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:39.600
back to what I was saying before about music
affecting different parts of the brain on

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:41.170
a physical level.

00:17:41.170 --> 00:17:42.170
Right.

00:17:42.170 --> 00:17:47.940
That part Ed, of the brain is being reached
as well as the sort of prefrontal-

00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:53.480
It's not just the brain stem for rhythm, its
auditory cortex, it's motor cortex, it's the

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:59.320
basal ganglia, and these parts of the brain
have intrinsic rhythms, and there's a lot

00:17:59.320 --> 00:18:00.910
we don't know about the brain.

00:18:00.910 --> 00:18:07.200
But one of the things we do know, is that
it communicates within itself via rhythms,

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:10.360
and different kinds of oscillations that are
going on.

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:15.430
And one of the most, I think amazing discoveries
in the past 20 years is that when we listen

00:18:15.430 --> 00:18:20.740
to musical rhythm, our brains synchronize
those intrinsic rhythms to the rhythms of

00:18:20.740 --> 00:18:21.740
music.

00:18:21.740 --> 00:18:24.660
That's the process known as entrainment.

00:18:24.660 --> 00:18:26.100
Entrainment, yeah.

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:30.200
Does your musical example give us a sense
of that?

00:18:30.200 --> 00:18:34.110
Yeah, so I picked on that, yeah would exactly
show that off.

00:18:34.110 --> 00:18:37.970
It happens to be, so one of the things that
happens when we listen to rhythmic music,

00:18:37.970 --> 00:18:42.990
it happens to everybody, is you'll find yourself
tapping your foot or bobbing your head.

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:48.970
This and that feature of music that makes
you want to move has been termed groove by

00:18:48.970 --> 00:18:53.540
one of our colleagues, Peter [Ginotta 00:21:50],
and he did a large study of all different

00:18:53.540 --> 00:18:58.730
kinds of older music and newer music, in an
undergraduate's rate the groove, and this

00:18:58.730 --> 00:19:03.520
piece that I chose was the one that they consistently
chose as number one.

00:19:03.520 --> 00:19:08.550
And I'd do this experiment in my class every
year, my undergraduates consistently choose

00:19:08.550 --> 00:19:11.410
it as number one, even those it's from the
seventies.

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:15.520
It's Superstition by Stevie Wonder.

00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:22.630
I see you.

00:19:22.630 --> 00:19:36.840
It's impossible not to move, right?

00:19:36.840 --> 00:19:38.720
When the clavinet comes in-

00:19:38.720 --> 00:19:39.720
Yeah.

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:43.320
You know, and adds its layer to the groove
that's already been established.

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:47.480
And it's just like, then the brain is suddenly
like, what just happened?

00:19:47.480 --> 00:19:49.640
Yeah, right right, right right.

00:19:49.640 --> 00:19:56.210
What would that look like David on, if you
would've hooked one of Ed's undergrads up

00:19:56.210 --> 00:19:59.150
to an FMRI, while that was happening?

00:19:59.150 --> 00:20:00.150
Any idea?

00:20:00.150 --> 00:20:03.290
We can take any of you and hook you up.

00:20:03.290 --> 00:20:09.100
Not so much an FMRI, FMRI is a wonderful camera,
it's a camera that takes pictures with very

00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:14.540
high spatial resolution, but to capture this
kind of thing you need a machine that actually

00:20:14.540 --> 00:20:17.910
has a high temper resolution, so you can follow
their rhythm.

00:20:17.910 --> 00:20:19.520
So FMRI's not so great.

00:20:19.520 --> 00:20:26.010
You want something like EEG, which many of
us use, or its big cousin magnetoencephalography,

00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:28.320
that's a giant recorder of brain activity.

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:33.580
And what you see, and what we can measure
is how different parts of the brain entrain.

00:20:33.580 --> 00:20:40.060
So the brain is hijacked by rhythmicity, you
can't stop yourself, and you see the brain

00:20:40.060 --> 00:20:46.760
activity, the waves of the brain surfing on
the waves of the sound, and it's sort of inescapable.

00:20:46.760 --> 00:20:51.680
So the sound can be kind of conceptualized
as a series of waves that we can analyze in

00:20:51.680 --> 00:20:56.720
all kinds of ways, and the brain activity
literally surfs on top of that, whether it

00:20:56.720 --> 00:20:57.770
wants to or not.

00:20:57.770 --> 00:21:00.110
So you're actually seeing movement, motions-

00:21:00.110 --> 00:21:03.850
Well you see, that's how we visual it.

00:21:03.850 --> 00:21:08.530
So Ed mentioned that the rhythmic brain activity
in different areas, or we call them neural

00:21:08.530 --> 00:21:12.460
oscillations, and you can imagine that is
the brain breathing at different rates.

00:21:12.460 --> 00:21:13.460
Yeah.

00:21:13.460 --> 00:21:14.460
Right?

00:21:14.460 --> 00:21:17.690
So, at the level of single cells or local
networks or larger networks, the brain is

00:21:17.690 --> 00:21:23.260
breathing at faster rates and slower rates,
it has to do with biological factoids that

00:21:23.260 --> 00:21:27.040
we don't have to unpack here for fear of losing
your audience.

00:21:27.040 --> 00:21:33.450
But these are sort of optimally suited to
grab onto the rhythmicity to the piece, and

00:21:33.450 --> 00:21:37.450
when you're listening to Superstition, different
parts from the periphery to the central part

00:21:37.450 --> 00:21:42.720
of the brain, literally are just locking on
or entraining to this, and so you can't stop

00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:43.720
yourself.

00:21:43.720 --> 00:21:47.410
How much of this Meagan, do you think is cultural?

00:21:47.410 --> 00:21:55.730
Would an Australian Aborigine in the Outback
be as quick to entrain to the groove of a

00:21:55.730 --> 00:22:01.550
Stevie Wonder as anybody who grew up with
Western pop music?

00:22:01.550 --> 00:22:03.220
Well, perhaps.

00:22:03.220 --> 00:22:07.350
There is some evidence that the emotions that
are perceived in music, are perceived to a

00:22:07.350 --> 00:22:09.870
certain extent at a universal level.

00:22:09.870 --> 00:22:15.120
So, if you are an individual from a particular
culture, you're going to be better at identifying

00:22:15.120 --> 00:22:19.930
emotion in music from your culture, than you
would for other cultures that have music that

00:22:19.930 --> 00:22:21.330
you're less familiar with.

00:22:21.330 --> 00:22:27.220
However, you'd still identify the emotion
above chance, and what that tells us is that

00:22:27.220 --> 00:22:32.050
there are some universal features that are
used to detect emotion in music.

00:22:32.050 --> 00:22:37.650
When we think about emotional responses to
music, it's actually helpful to distinguish

00:22:37.650 --> 00:22:43.180
between the effective response that you have,
and what is being communicated by the music.

00:22:43.180 --> 00:22:50.040
And, within a given culture, there are these
codes that exist, that tell the listener that

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:55.770
this is a sad song, or this is a happy song,
or this song is going to induce fear.

00:22:55.770 --> 00:23:00.740
And those codes are fairly well established
and agreed upon by listeners within a culture.

00:23:00.740 --> 00:23:06.640
So one of the interesting things that you'll
see is that listeners will very quickly identify

00:23:06.640 --> 00:23:11.720
an emotion that's communicated by the music,
they could do so after hearing only a quarter

00:23:11.720 --> 00:23:17.700
of a second of a song, and the level of agreement
between listeners is actually really high

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:19.810
particularly within cultures.

00:23:19.810 --> 00:23:25.420
Now, how that translates to your emotional
experience, the emotions that you feel in

00:23:25.420 --> 00:23:28.380
response to the song, that's a different question.

00:23:28.380 --> 00:23:32.450
So you have what's being communicated by the
music, and then you have the factors that

00:23:32.450 --> 00:23:35.360
lead to you actually experiencing an emotion.

00:23:35.360 --> 00:23:39.171
And a field really makes a distinction between
the two.

00:23:39.171 --> 00:23:45.650
You sound like you're probably familiar with
the work of Thomas Fritz among the Mafa people.

00:23:45.650 --> 00:23:53.590
Where he ... the Mafa live in the highlands
of Cameroon and had minimal contact with the

00:23:53.590 --> 00:23:55.490
West and Western music.

00:23:55.490 --> 00:24:02.261
And he went there with a series of pieces
of music had a happy face, a sad face, and

00:24:02.261 --> 00:24:09.650
a scared face, and consistently, the Mafa
were able to, hearing Western music for the

00:24:09.650 --> 00:24:14.620
first time, or keyboard music for the first
time that he had brought to them, were consistently

00:24:14.620 --> 00:24:17.690
able to identify correctly the emotion.

00:24:17.690 --> 00:24:22.890
And then even more unusually he brought some
Mafa music back to Germany, and the people

00:24:22.890 --> 00:24:30.210
there were able to identify correctly the
emotions that music was carrying.

00:24:30.210 --> 00:24:35.090
I wonder for you Mari, did you grow up with
traditional Japanese music as well as Western

00:24:35.090 --> 00:24:36.090
Yeah.

00:24:36.090 --> 00:24:41.250
When I grow up in Japan, I was telling your
producer that there's a song, children's song,

00:24:41.250 --> 00:24:50.950
it's a very innocent about bubbles.

00:24:50.950 --> 00:24:53.310
Right?

00:24:53.310 --> 00:24:59.390
It's like a happy song.

00:24:59.390 --> 00:25:00.390
Yeah.

00:25:00.390 --> 00:25:07.030
It's a song about little bubbles, you're blowing
bubbles, and bubbles fly to the roof.

00:25:07.030 --> 00:25:15.650
Oh it hit the ceiling and he disappears, oh
we don't blow, let the bubbles fly.

00:25:15.650 --> 00:25:18.740
And it's just an innocent song.

00:25:18.740 --> 00:25:24.960
And then it turned out the composer's daughter
died, so the lyrics are about the daughter

00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:29.440
who died, he said don't kill my daughter.

00:25:29.440 --> 00:25:33.420
After I knew that, I cannot listen to it the
same way, right?

00:25:33.420 --> 00:25:34.420
Yeah.

00:25:34.420 --> 00:25:40.620
So it's very like, I grew up singing that
song, but now I can't.

00:25:40.620 --> 00:25:42.160
I relate in a different way.

00:25:42.160 --> 00:25:49.210
Okay, so this gets to an interesting question,
which is, let's say just for sake of argument,

00:25:49.210 --> 00:25:54.220
that and I know this isn't true, but let's
just say, everybody who grew up on Western

00:25:54.220 --> 00:25:59.410
pop music, hearing the opening of Superstition,
is going to get that little pop of dopamine

00:25:59.410 --> 00:26:00.410
or whatever.

00:26:00.410 --> 00:26:05.640
That little hit, that little shiver down the
spine, when that keyboard comes in.

00:26:05.640 --> 00:26:12.740
Would that happen when that listener is two
or three, or does that listener have to have

00:26:12.740 --> 00:26:18.900
some experience to know ... I mean at what
point does your own cultural and individual

00:26:18.900 --> 00:26:25.000
experience completely alter or color your
response to music?

00:26:25.000 --> 00:26:31.340
So it's interesting, and a lot of people make
this mistake, they think, well you know, I

00:26:31.340 --> 00:26:35.690
sit down and listen to music, I start tapping
my foot immediately, therefore it's innate,

00:26:35.690 --> 00:26:38.270
it's inborn somehow.

00:26:38.270 --> 00:26:45.010
But in fact, there is a long trajectory to
the development of rhythm perception and rhythmic

00:26:45.010 --> 00:26:46.610
interaction with music.

00:26:46.610 --> 00:26:52.240
So, turns out infants, if you test infants
the day after they're born, you play them

00:26:52.240 --> 00:26:57.240
different kinds of sounds, you play them speech,
you play them music, they'll move more to

00:26:57.240 --> 00:27:02.060
music, but they won't synchronize, they can't
synchronize.

00:27:02.060 --> 00:27:10.800
It turns out that if you play them rhythms
from the Western culture, and Balkan cultures

00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:15.000
like Bulgaria, they can tell the difference
between those rhythms when they're six months

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:19.410
old, but by the time they're 12 months old,
they undergo perceptual narrowing, and now

00:27:19.410 --> 00:27:20.820
they can't make those distinctions.

00:27:20.820 --> 00:27:26.370
Similarly, to speech or to faces.

00:27:26.370 --> 00:27:29.060
Kids that are two and a half, three and a
half, and four and a half years old still

00:27:29.060 --> 00:27:31.940
can't synchronize very well.

00:27:31.940 --> 00:27:35.840
But they synchronize better if there's a person
in the room to synchronize with.

00:27:35.840 --> 00:27:42.540
And it's, they're six or seven years old before
most kids can really dance in synchrony with

00:27:42.540 --> 00:27:43.540
music.

00:27:43.540 --> 00:27:44.900
There're exceptions, right?

00:27:44.900 --> 00:27:46.080
Right, right.

00:27:46.080 --> 00:27:47.840
Justin Bieber being one, right?

00:27:47.840 --> 00:27:49.450
You've seen the movies of him playing-

00:27:49.450 --> 00:27:52.100
You had to go there, didn't you Ed?

00:27:52.100 --> 00:27:53.100
Yeah I had to.

00:27:53.100 --> 00:27:56.740
But there're sort of rhythmic geniuses out
there.

00:27:56.740 --> 00:28:01.260
But most kids, they're six or seven before
they can really synchronize with music.

00:28:01.260 --> 00:28:06.360
But, even to come back to what David, you
were saying about Mari's piece, Alt, that

00:28:06.360 --> 00:28:13.360
you heard something in it that she probably
hadn't intended, but three or four year old

00:28:13.360 --> 00:28:19.830
David Poeppel might not have known what to
make of that.

00:28:19.830 --> 00:28:22.780
You're bringing a lifetime of auditory experience.

00:28:22.780 --> 00:28:30.010
Of course you bring one of the delicious parts
of human experiences that we have a hard disc

00:28:30.010 --> 00:28:32.830
of life in our head, that colors everything.

00:28:32.830 --> 00:28:39.130
So what we bring to every experience, whether
it's music, the visual arts, dance, theater,

00:28:39.130 --> 00:28:44.470
what the art historian Hans Gombrich called
The Beholder's Share, it's I think a beautiful

00:28:44.470 --> 00:28:45.470
phrase.

00:28:45.470 --> 00:28:50.990
And so for me, the Beholder's Share to to
you piece was finding something different.

00:28:50.990 --> 00:28:57.020
I don't wanna skew this discussion too much
towards meaning, but I will anyway.

00:28:57.020 --> 00:29:01.650
Because a second example that just came up,
was the one you gave with the Japanese children's

00:29:01.650 --> 00:29:07.360
song, which you hear it for the first time
and think, oh that has a cheerful thing.

00:29:07.360 --> 00:29:11.310
The moment you tell the story, it colors it
in a completely different way.

00:29:11.310 --> 00:29:18.140
So now, I too, cannot hear this, 'cause it's
kind of, oh this is this joyful rah, rah the

00:29:18.140 --> 00:29:21.310
balloons are flying up or blowing bubbles.

00:29:21.310 --> 00:29:27.430
Knowledge is a dangerous thing, in knowledge
your preconceived, the priors you bring with

00:29:27.430 --> 00:29:32.660
you to the perceptual experience color your
interpretation of that.

00:29:32.660 --> 00:29:37.400
That invariably leads to individual differences
because we all have slightly different things

00:29:37.400 --> 00:29:38.860
on our hard disc.

00:29:38.860 --> 00:29:47.700
And your hard disc, includes a little area
devoted to the music of Tom Waits, which …

00:29:47.700 --> 00:29:50.730
Surprisingly big area.

00:29:50.730 --> 00:30:00.320
So I am to the dismay of my wife and sons
who are all musicians incidentally, I am a

00:30:00.320 --> 00:30:05.010
deep fan of Tom Waits, and I selected a song
for the particular reason I think it makes

00:30:05.010 --> 00:30:15.890
a few interesting points, called Kentucky
Avenue, which is about childhood

00:30:15.890 --> 00:30:31.900
and pairs well with your-

00:30:31.900 --> 00:30:35.900
[music]

00:30:35.900 --> 00:30:37.780
So this one works for me every time.

00:30:37.780 --> 00:30:40.300
Okay, why did I?

00:30:40.300 --> 00:30:43.280
I selected this simply because I like it.

00:30:43.280 --> 00:30:47.090
But it makes a couple of points that I think
are germane to our discussion.

00:30:47.090 --> 00:30:50.040
One is the, so this is about childhood right?

00:30:50.040 --> 00:30:56.160
This is a song about all the weird stuff that
happens when you're a kid, and the compelling

00:30:56.160 --> 00:30:57.390
effect it has on your head.

00:30:57.390 --> 00:31:02.410
So the stuff, the first time you heard certain
things, or you fell in love, or you kissed

00:31:02.410 --> 00:31:05.960
for the first time, or you had some awful
experience with a parent, anything.

00:31:05.960 --> 00:31:11.140
Stuff in your childhood has a particularly
compelling grip on things.

00:31:11.140 --> 00:31:17.230
That's one point, so our experiences early
on, really color stuff later very profoundly.

00:31:17.230 --> 00:31:21.660
The second point I think is, which is interesting,
and I think it's easy to overlook, although

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:27.710
we all have this experience, is that material
that's putatively sad, which this song is,

00:31:27.710 --> 00:31:30.380
can give you a positive aesthetic experience,
right?

00:31:30.380 --> 00:31:35.350
So sad things are supposed to be sad, and
in real life, sad things are sad, we don't

00:31:35.350 --> 00:31:37.940
seek bad experiences, we seek positive experiences.

00:31:37.940 --> 00:31:45.030
But in the arts, one of the fascinating things
is that a negatively colored emotional things,

00:31:45.030 --> 00:31:50.030
can be experienced as wonderful, and aesthetically
engaging and moving, and that's one of the

00:31:50.030 --> 00:31:54.470
most exciting things about the arts, and the
music really brings it out in this magnificent

00:31:54.470 --> 00:31:55.470
way.

00:31:55.470 --> 00:31:56.470
Yeah.

00:31:56.470 --> 00:32:01.160
Meagan, you've done a lot of work with this
sort of, the behavioral kind of response is.

00:32:01.160 --> 00:32:04.570
What is it about the sad song that we keep
coming back to?

00:32:04.570 --> 00:32:09.280
Well, it might be that it's one of the safe
ways that we can experience this emotion,

00:32:09.280 --> 00:32:14.170
without having any sort of personal consequences.

00:32:14.170 --> 00:32:19.910
When you have sadness that's evoked through
traditional ecological means, it's because

00:32:19.910 --> 00:32:23.620
you've experienced a personal loss, no one
wants that.

00:32:23.620 --> 00:32:28.010
But music, and art in general, gives us the
chance to explore these emotions that we tend

00:32:28.010 --> 00:32:32.110
to avoid, because they have no consequences
in this context.

00:32:32.110 --> 00:32:36.550
So we can go to the theater and enjoy being
really frightened, because we know that we're

00:32:36.550 --> 00:32:41.070
not in danger, and music may be doing the
same type of thing, where it gives us the

00:32:41.070 --> 00:32:46.570
chance to explore negative emotions, in a
way that have no consequences for us.

00:32:46.570 --> 00:32:52.010
Yeah, and I think that it's really about experiencing
a certain beautiful thing together, right?

00:32:52.010 --> 00:32:53.480
The fact we're in it together.

00:32:53.480 --> 00:33:04.230
I think that a lot of the experience of music
moves us because it's kind of a resonant emotional

00:33:04.230 --> 00:33:05.910
experience across a lot of people.

00:33:05.910 --> 00:33:11.640
And so, we've done some work looking at brain
differences between people who often get chills

00:33:11.640 --> 00:33:16.020
when they listen to music, compared to people
who don't, when controlling for all these

00:33:16.020 --> 00:33:20.530
different variables, and it seems like the
auditory areas are actually more connected

00:33:20.530 --> 00:33:24.820
to areas of the brain that are important for
social and emotional processing, among people

00:33:24.820 --> 00:33:26.610
who always get chills.

00:33:26.610 --> 00:33:32.120
So it's as if, when you're listening to something
that's beautiful, it's a songwriter's way

00:33:32.120 --> 00:33:38.330
of going straight to your social and emotional
processing regions of the brain, through an

00:33:38.330 --> 00:33:39.380
auditory channel.

00:33:39.380 --> 00:33:44.320
So Meagan, the music that you chose for us
is a sad song.

00:33:44.320 --> 00:33:45.480
It is.

00:33:45.480 --> 00:33:53.010
But it is a very different kind of sad song,
from Tom Waits lonely guy at the bar at closing

00:33:53.010 --> 00:33:59.870
time sort of, this is, I'm sad and I'm storming
the gates of heaven to let the universe know

00:33:59.870 --> 00:34:02.220
it.

00:34:02.220 --> 00:34:07.640
What is it about this excerpt from the Mozart
Requiem that does it for you?

00:34:07.640 --> 00:34:13.490
So, one of things that happens in the clip
that we're going to hear, is that Mozart created

00:34:13.490 --> 00:34:18.740
this beautiful melodic line, that doesn't
resolve in a way that you expect it to.

00:34:18.740 --> 00:34:25.159
So the end of the melodic line ends on the
seventh scale degree, and we generally expect

00:34:25.159 --> 00:34:29.861
that to resolve to a very stable scale degree
in music, that we call the tonic, and that

00:34:29.861 --> 00:34:30.940
doesn't happen.

00:34:30.940 --> 00:34:36.550
And I think that leaves the listener wanting
more, and it gives you this sense of lack

00:34:36.550 --> 00:34:44.870
of fulfillment, yet there's such beauty in
that, and there's mystery, and for me it definitely

00:34:44.870 --> 00:34:46.630
causes all sorts of emotions.

00:34:46.630 --> 00:35:10.060
Well this is some of the Lacrimosa from the
Mozart Requiem.

00:35:10.060 --> 00:35:33.540
I suddenly wanna buy a car.

00:35:33.540 --> 00:35:41.190
It's been used in a car commercial recently.

00:35:41.190 --> 00:35:42.840
Which is pretty telling actually.

00:35:42.840 --> 00:35:43.840
You know?

00:35:43.840 --> 00:35:46.150
You had your emotional experience.

00:35:46.150 --> 00:35:47.150
Longing, longing.

00:35:47.150 --> 00:35:49.540
What kind of car?

00:35:49.540 --> 00:35:53.550
I don't remember the brand, but it's a recent
commercial.

00:35:53.550 --> 00:36:00.530
But this piece has been used in films, many
... That, especially what we heard at the

00:36:00.530 --> 00:36:07.130
end, where the voices come back in, that's
become a kind of iconic musical moment in

00:36:07.130 --> 00:36:13.760
film, in TV shows, music consultants love
this piece.

00:36:13.760 --> 00:36:21.320
Because it has a lot of emotional content,
not necessarily the one that the text would

00:36:21.320 --> 00:36:25.270
lead you to expect, Lacrimosa is tears in
Latin.

00:36:25.270 --> 00:36:33.120
But this, people hear something grand in this,
and something elegant, and so you said there're

00:36:33.120 --> 00:36:35.080
lots of emotion in this.

00:36:35.080 --> 00:36:36.080
Right.

00:36:36.080 --> 00:36:37.080
For you, what do you hear?

00:36:37.080 --> 00:36:42.070
Well, one of the interesting things about
this melodic line is that for me it reminds

00:36:42.070 --> 00:36:45.590
me of the sound of someone crying.

00:36:45.590 --> 00:36:52.580
So the fact that it is Lacrimosa tears, may
have some direct relevance to how he decided

00:36:52.580 --> 00:36:53.690
to create that line.

00:36:53.690 --> 00:36:59.680
Maybe he was trying to emulate the types of
sounds that people create when they're crying.

00:36:59.680 --> 00:37:07.550
So, for me this taps into this very basic
type of sadness response, and yet it's not

00:37:07.550 --> 00:37:15.320
my sadness, so for me that becomes translated
into an aesthetic response, rather than me

00:37:15.320 --> 00:37:20.520
becoming personally sad, instead I appreciate
the beauty of it.

00:37:20.520 --> 00:37:26.960
So, if you're feeling a kind of response on
a purely aesthetic level, are you less likely

00:37:26.960 --> 00:37:32.540
to get that shiver-effect, than if you're
actually responding to it as if that's your

00:37:32.540 --> 00:37:34.750
emotion that's being prodded?

00:37:34.750 --> 00:37:40.180
Well the shiver is thought to be an aesthetic
response.

00:37:40.180 --> 00:37:47.040
Oftentimes the songs that are most able to
create the shiver-response are sad songs.

00:37:47.040 --> 00:37:54.460
Yet people say that the response is highly
positive, people generally don't think that

00:37:54.460 --> 00:37:59.930
they're feeling sadness when they experience
this extreme emotional response.

00:37:59.930 --> 00:38:04.970
So, what is being communicated by the piece,
maybe very different than what is being experienced

00:38:04.970 --> 00:38:05.970
by the listener.

00:38:05.970 --> 00:38:10.630
The listener response, maybe highly pleasurable,
even though the piece is really sad.

00:38:10.630 --> 00:38:16.880
And actually that kind of brings us to the
piece that I chose, which is something I've

00:38:16.880 --> 00:38:24.770
only recently discovered, and which I'm not
sure I know how to set up, other than to say,

00:38:24.770 --> 00:38:29.190
it's a man and a woman in a room, in their
living room.

00:38:29.190 --> 00:38:35.040
Bruce Green and Cora Loy McWhirter, I think
they're married, they're in North Carolina,

00:38:35.040 --> 00:38:37.870
and they sing old folk songs.

00:38:37.870 --> 00:38:45.040
Like generally lesser known pretty dark, what
the critic Real Mark has called, weird old

00:38:45.040 --> 00:38:46.040
America.

00:38:46.040 --> 00:38:59.090
And it's one microphone, there's no reverb,
it's just two voices singing, I'll say together,

00:38:59.090 --> 00:39:00.200
but you can decide for yourself.

00:39:00.200 --> 00:39:33.040
The song is called, Come Near My Love.So,

00:39:33.040 --> 00:39:36.580
I don't know what the hell is happening there.

00:39:36.580 --> 00:39:38.140
Harmony.

00:39:38.140 --> 00:39:39.300
But what kind?

00:39:39.300 --> 00:39:41.790
It's a fourth.

00:39:41.790 --> 00:39:42.790
Parallelfourth.

00:39:42.790 --> 00:39:46.220
But it's not tempered, it's not the parallel
fourth that get-

00:39:46.220 --> 00:39:49.430
Sometimes it's not parallel, maybe that's
what you mean.

00:39:49.430 --> 00:39:52.260
They sound like they're droning first of all.

00:39:52.260 --> 00:39:53.260
Yeah.

00:39:53.260 --> 00:39:54.260
Rather than singing.

00:39:54.260 --> 00:39:55.950
The sound of the fourth, right?

00:39:55.950 --> 00:39:56.950
Yeah.

00:39:56.950 --> 00:40:04.940
So, again it's not a tempered, it's a pure
... So it's wrong, it's not the fourth that

00:40:04.940 --> 00:40:10.700
you'll get at a piano or a guitar, although
you could do it.

00:40:10.700 --> 00:40:12.680
Pythagorean.

00:40:12.680 --> 00:40:14.670
maybe.

00:40:14.670 --> 00:40:15.670
I don't know.

00:40:15.670 --> 00:40:19.590
I'm often listening to music while I'm doing
other things, and I just stuck this CD in

00:40:19.590 --> 00:40:25.690
'cause a friend recommended it, and I just
like rooted to the spot, what is that?

00:40:25.690 --> 00:40:36.490
Because it was unexpected, and just like different
from anything that I'd heard from Mountain

00:40:36.490 --> 00:40:41.130
Appalachian, the high lonesome sound, it's
definitely not that.

00:40:41.130 --> 00:40:46.480
It's definitely not Nashville style harmony,
it was just really kind of unexpected.

00:40:46.480 --> 00:40:51.200
Well you know, it's funny, you started out
the conversation by saying, well you know

00:40:51.200 --> 00:40:56.900
30 years ago there was no music neuroscience
and so forth, but really music has been studied

00:40:56.900 --> 00:40:59.080
in science since there was science.

00:40:59.080 --> 00:41:00.810
At least in the West.

00:41:00.810 --> 00:41:02.370
Mari brought up Pythagoras.

00:41:02.370 --> 00:41:08.370
Pythagoras was considered by some the first
scientist in the West, and his question was

00:41:08.370 --> 00:41:09.740
exactly that.

00:41:09.740 --> 00:41:14.540
Why do those two notes sound so good together?

00:41:14.540 --> 00:41:16.380
And we're still asking the question.

00:41:16.380 --> 00:41:17.380
Right.

00:41:17.380 --> 00:41:23.290
And why we should not let these two notes
play together, because they make people do

00:41:23.290 --> 00:41:26.180
impure things.

00:41:26.180 --> 00:41:29.070
That's right.

00:41:29.070 --> 00:41:30.070
That lasted-

00:41:30.070 --> 00:41:31.070
Til the 15th-

00:41:31.070 --> 00:41:40.640
In the 18th century, Johan Mathisson in Germany,
a doctrine of the different affects that scales

00:41:40.640 --> 00:41:45.890
would ... this scale will make people slit
their wrists, this people will make people

00:41:45.890 --> 00:41:54.130
run out and make bad decisions with the next
person of the opposite sex that they meet.

00:41:54.130 --> 00:41:59.180
This was really, this was seen as science,
and you know.

00:41:59.180 --> 00:42:05.880
Well you know, I think a lot thinking like
that went way off the tracks.

00:42:05.880 --> 00:42:06.880
Right?

00:42:06.880 --> 00:42:15.130
But there's also a lot of good evidence that
there are physiological responses that we

00:42:15.130 --> 00:42:16.130
have.

00:42:16.130 --> 00:42:21.850
The brain, going back to the brain stem, responses
in the brain stem in the early auditory system,

00:42:21.850 --> 00:42:27.420
that correspond to our feelings of what this
harmony makes us feel.

00:42:27.420 --> 00:42:32.310
You can almost, in that song you played, you
can almost just hear one harmony, it doesn't

00:42:32.310 --> 00:42:37.950
even need to progress, and you can already
feel an emotionality out of that.

00:42:37.950 --> 00:42:43.990
So I don't think, although it has for the
past couple of 100 years been very fashionable

00:42:43.990 --> 00:42:49.640
to discount that way of thinking, I think
people are beginning to come back and say,

00:42:49.640 --> 00:42:54.230
no maybe there really is something about the
way we respond to individual intervals that's

00:42:54.230 --> 00:42:55.230
really important.

00:42:55.230 --> 00:42:57.310
All right, so give me some news you can use.

00:42:57.310 --> 00:43:05.400
Why is this important to study, what does
it tell us, what if any use is this?

00:43:05.400 --> 00:43:08.360
Scientifically, behaviorally.

00:43:08.360 --> 00:43:10.810
You mean music in general?

00:43:10.810 --> 00:43:16.010
No, our exploration of these kinds of emotional
and the physical affect.

00:43:16.010 --> 00:43:18.060
Look, I think we shouldn't have to justify
this.

00:43:18.060 --> 00:43:21.700
So let me disagree with the question.

00:43:21.700 --> 00:43:22.800
The premise of the question.

00:43:22.800 --> 00:43:24.060
So obviously there's a-

00:43:24.060 --> 00:43:26.680
It is a world science festival.

00:43:26.680 --> 00:43:35.660
So yes, that's why we do science, because
we can't help ourself trying to understand

00:43:35.660 --> 00:43:36.660
stuff.

00:43:36.660 --> 00:43:39.250
And so there's a deep value to basic research.

00:43:39.250 --> 00:43:41.470
There has to be no nugget.

00:43:41.470 --> 00:43:46.230
So it turns out that there is, because there's
fascinating therapeutic implications understanding

00:43:46.230 --> 00:43:47.230
music.

00:43:47.230 --> 00:43:51.530
But from my point of view, we do basic science
to simply understand the parts list, we're

00:43:51.530 --> 00:43:55.490
here, we're trying to understand the parts
list of the mind, the brain, and music.

00:43:55.490 --> 00:44:00.310
And so, there are two things that can happen,
we can use to understand something about music,

00:44:00.310 --> 00:44:04.880
and something about the mind and brain, and
we can use neural biological methods to understand

00:44:04.880 --> 00:44:05.880
something about music.

00:44:05.880 --> 00:44:10.510
These are perfectly valuable and reasonable
things, and there has to be no end game, if

00:44:10.510 --> 00:44:16.420
there's a pill or a lotion or a car I can
buy, it matters not.

00:44:16.420 --> 00:44:17.420
Okay?

00:44:17.420 --> 00:44:22.100
We do basic science because we're wired to
have ... we have a science forming faculty,

00:44:22.100 --> 00:44:23.830
we just try to understand stuff.

00:44:23.830 --> 00:44:28.810
And that's very important, and we should not
forget that part of science is just that.

00:44:28.810 --> 00:44:35.610
It's not trying to come up with an application,
it's to satisfy curiosity and to let our minds

00:44:35.610 --> 00:44:39.070
wander in weird directions, and say, damn
that was interesting.

00:44:39.070 --> 00:44:44.700
It turns out music is an extremely well-structured
domain to learn something about how the mind

00:44:44.700 --> 00:44:45.700
and brain are organized.

00:44:45.700 --> 00:44:47.640
And that's deeply exciting and satisfying.

00:44:47.640 --> 00:44:48.640
Yeah.

00:44:48.640 --> 00:44:50.270
I can't agree with that more.

00:44:50.270 --> 00:44:55.730
But I also think that the reason why I do
science is so that I can understand more things,

00:44:55.730 --> 00:44:59.570
and so that I could be more open-minded towards
new ideas.

00:44:59.570 --> 00:45:02.900
Before I started doing this, it was like of
course you listen to Mozart, because he was

00:45:02.900 --> 00:45:07.130
a genius, because he sounded good, and he
was good because of all these ways.

00:45:07.130 --> 00:45:11.820
But then you hear about all the people who
are kind of deriving different experiences

00:45:11.820 --> 00:45:15.920
from the same thing, like the illusion.

00:45:15.920 --> 00:45:20.050
And then that, thinking about that just makes
you appreciate like diversity more, it makes

00:45:20.050 --> 00:45:22.500
you more open-minded I think.

00:45:22.500 --> 00:45:26.880
Meagan, do you more of an application-based
approach?

00:45:26.880 --> 00:45:32.340
Well I think that addressing this question
in general it raises new questions about the

00:45:32.340 --> 00:45:36.760
nature of music, and the significance to the
human species.

00:45:36.760 --> 00:45:42.640
So if you look at the brain areas that have
the strongest response when someone is experiencing

00:45:42.640 --> 00:45:47.110
chills, you see that they are the same brain
areas that are involved in rewarding behavior

00:45:47.110 --> 00:45:49.050
that has biological significance.

00:45:49.050 --> 00:45:55.120
So acquiring food, having sex, and these brain
areas are also responsive to drugs.

00:45:55.120 --> 00:46:00.951
So, when we see that music is activating these
reward centers, we have to ask, well, does

00:46:00.951 --> 00:46:04.610
it have any sort of biological value for humans?

00:46:04.610 --> 00:46:08.690
Or, is it hijacking these systems in the same
way that drugs do?

00:46:08.690 --> 00:46:09.790
And that's an open question.

00:46:09.790 --> 00:46:13.540
But I think this type of research raises those
questions, and those questions are important

00:46:13.540 --> 00:46:17.600
to understanding what is music, and why is
it important to humans?

00:46:17.600 --> 00:46:18.800
How about you Ed?

00:46:18.800 --> 00:46:24.860
Well I want to start out by agreeing with
David, because I'm 100% I think he's right

00:46:24.860 --> 00:46:26.010
on with that.

00:46:26.010 --> 00:46:28.600
I think, but I wanna add two things to it.

00:46:28.600 --> 00:46:33.730
One he touched on, which is with music, music
is so well-structured that it allows us to

00:46:33.730 --> 00:46:41.501
go and see the brain when we use EEG or EMG,
we can see with the rhythms of music, we can

00:46:41.501 --> 00:46:45.920
see so clearly that brain entrainment.

00:46:45.920 --> 00:46:50.170
People like David have spent a lot of time
studying speech, and it's in some ways the

00:46:50.170 --> 00:46:55.470
structure is not quite as clear, but in music
we're able to first go in and say, oh my gosh

00:46:55.470 --> 00:46:57.840
the brain is really entraining to music.

00:46:57.840 --> 00:47:01.580
And then, pretty soon, people like David were
saying, hey guess what, it's happening for

00:47:01.580 --> 00:47:04.480
speech too.

00:47:04.480 --> 00:47:11.770
So it gives us a kind of a way to focus the
brain on this rhythmic thing.

00:47:11.770 --> 00:47:14.890
And then I will go and talk about some applications.

00:47:14.890 --> 00:47:23.010
So it turns out that there're a lot of kind
of disorders of the brain in which you find

00:47:23.010 --> 00:47:25.440
people also have a problem with rhythm.

00:47:25.440 --> 00:47:32.830
Okay, so autistic kids have a problem with
rhythm, people with specific language impairment,

00:47:32.830 --> 00:47:35.400
people with dyslexia.

00:47:35.400 --> 00:47:40.820
And there're other ways in which ... So we're
not exactly quite sure what that means yet,

00:47:40.820 --> 00:47:45.800
but there're other very clear ways in which
people are using music therapy to clinical

00:47:45.800 --> 00:47:51.090
benefits, so to help rehabilitate speech after
a stroke for example, with melodic intonation

00:47:51.090 --> 00:47:58.680
therapy or rhythmic stimulation can help people
rehabilitate their gait, if they have Parkinson's

00:47:58.680 --> 00:47:59.680
Disease.

00:47:59.680 --> 00:48:06.360
So there really are, we are beginning to understand
that there may actually be some clinical applications

00:48:06.360 --> 00:48:10.790
to music, and a lot of them have to do with
rhythm.

00:48:10.790 --> 00:48:13.971
It's funny that we're coming to this realization
at a time when there're a lot of musicians

00:48:13.971 --> 00:48:19.880
who kind of pushing at the borders of what
we consider to be music, as opposed to noise.

00:48:19.880 --> 00:48:26.150
That, we could go down a whole rabbit hole,
but instead, I think what we're going to do

00:48:26.150 --> 00:48:31.540
is open the floor to questions from you folks,
Yeah..

00:48:31.540 --> 00:48:38.190
In talking about the fundamental processes
involved in engaging with music, and talking

00:48:38.190 --> 00:48:41.920
about rhythmicity, that's suggests that if
it's so fundamental, that it would be happening

00:48:41.920 --> 00:48:43.960
in other species as well.

00:48:43.960 --> 00:48:48.780
So what kinds of properties here generalize,
that we can tell, generalize the experiences

00:48:48.780 --> 00:48:52.830
that say dogs or apes or others have with
music?

00:48:52.830 --> 00:48:54.410
Oh, that's a really great question.

00:48:54.410 --> 00:48:56.210
Is that Finn by the way?

00:48:56.210 --> 00:48:57.930
Hi Finn, how you doing?

00:48:57.930 --> 00:49:00.730
Did you plant a question in the audience?

00:49:00.730 --> 00:49:03.860
I did not plant a question, but I'm glad she
asked it.

00:49:03.860 --> 00:49:07.600
There're a bunch of really exciting studies
going on right now.

00:49:07.600 --> 00:49:13.730
I don't know, probably many of you in the
audience have seen Snowball, the dancing cockatoo?

00:49:13.730 --> 00:49:16.610
And people raise your hand, just so I get
an idea of, yeah.

00:49:16.610 --> 00:49:22.640
So you can go on YouTube, dances to the Backstreet
Boys, it's sort of, it looks like he's having

00:49:22.640 --> 00:49:24.090
a really great time.

00:49:24.090 --> 00:49:32.430
One of my colleagues Oni Patel or two, and
John Iverson went and actually studied Snowball,

00:49:32.430 --> 00:49:38.580
and found that Snowball is in fact doing a
pretty good job of synchronizing.

00:49:38.580 --> 00:49:44.119
It's intermittent synchronization, so Snowball
goes in and out, but statistically, they measured

00:49:44.119 --> 00:49:47.290
statistically, he's pretty well synchronized.

00:49:47.290 --> 00:49:58.040
But, so then another colleague of mine Peter
Cooke, decided to teach a sea lion to synchronize.

00:49:58.040 --> 00:50:06.220
And this is Ronin, who's also you could see
on the web, and Ronin synchronizes as well

00:50:06.220 --> 00:50:07.220
as a person.

00:50:07.220 --> 00:50:10.760
Literally, just never misses a beat.

00:50:10.760 --> 00:50:16.300
So there're some animals, we have done some
preliminary work with a Bonobo, just playing

00:50:16.300 --> 00:50:21.220
the drums and trying to get the Bonobo to
synchronize, and the Bonobo will synchronize

00:50:21.220 --> 00:50:22.369
with drum sounds.

00:50:22.369 --> 00:50:26.020
The thing about some of these other animals
though actually synchronize to music, which

00:50:26.020 --> 00:50:28.420
we haven't gotten there with the Bonobos yet.

00:50:28.420 --> 00:50:33.070
So, there're some people who say, well it's
really a human thing, or you really to be

00:50:33.070 --> 00:50:37.710
a vocal learning species, but then there're
other people, things like Ronin suggests,

00:50:37.710 --> 00:50:44.110
well, maybe it is more widespread, and at
least the capability to synchronize.

00:50:44.110 --> 00:50:46.030
Maybe not the motivation per se.

00:50:46.030 --> 00:50:47.030
But yeah.

00:50:47.030 --> 00:50:49.030
All right, let's take one more question.

00:50:49.030 --> 00:50:50.030
Yes?

00:50:50.030 --> 00:50:51.119
Gentleman in the blue shirt here.

00:50:51.119 --> 00:50:52.119
Hi.

00:50:52.119 --> 00:50:57.160
My mom has dementia, she's in her nineties,
in a conversation she may not remember what

00:50:57.160 --> 00:51:03.930
you said 30 seconds before, but she can recall
the old hymns, word for word, or if you put

00:51:03.930 --> 00:51:09.340
on Glenn Miller or Tommy Dorsey or something,
she's back out on the dance floor with my

00:51:09.340 --> 00:51:10.340
dad.

00:51:10.340 --> 00:51:14.250
What's going on there, and how can it help
people with Alzheimer's and dementia?

00:51:14.250 --> 00:51:16.930
Do you wanna talk about that?

00:51:16.930 --> 00:51:19.170
I'll say a little bit.

00:51:19.170 --> 00:51:24.610
We know we have this network in our brain
called the default network that's constantly

00:51:24.610 --> 00:51:25.800
on.

00:51:25.800 --> 00:51:30.210
And especially an area in the frontal lobe,
medial prefrontal cortex, tends to have less

00:51:30.210 --> 00:51:35.720
activity over time as you age, and people
with Alzheimer's it's really much less activity.

00:51:35.720 --> 00:51:42.090
And so what I think the music is doing is
kind of acting a rejuvenator of that system,

00:51:42.090 --> 00:51:47.030
so that you're increasing activity in those
regions that that might then last a little

00:51:47.030 --> 00:51:48.030
while.

00:51:48.030 --> 00:51:51.510
Yeah, I think you know, the brain is a funny
thing.

00:51:51.510 --> 00:51:55.190
And people talk about the brain as if it's
a computer, right?

00:51:55.190 --> 00:52:00.790
And we wanna know how this little cog works
with that little wheel and so forth.

00:52:00.790 --> 00:52:05.470
But if what we learn when we study the brain
it that it just doesn't work that way.

00:52:05.470 --> 00:52:08.190
It works in all kinds of counterintuitive
ways.

00:52:08.190 --> 00:52:12.630
Another thing that you might know is that,
if you have a stroke and you lose your ability

00:52:12.630 --> 00:52:18.450
to speak, there's a very good possibility
that you will preserve your ability to sing,

00:52:18.450 --> 00:52:23.530
and that's now being exploited in melodic
intonation therapy and so forth.

00:52:23.530 --> 00:52:24.530
So the answer is we don't really know what's
going on, but music is touching like every

00:52:24.530 --> 00:52:25.530
facet of memory and our ability to communicate
and so forth.

00:52:25.530 --> 00:52:31.130
And on that note, let me thank all of you
for being here on our wonderful panelists,

00:52:31.130 --> 00:52:39.450
thank you so much.

